# .promote-target — PARSED by scripts/promote_cli.py as key=value, never sourced.
#
# Copy to the repository root as `.promote-target` and fill in all three keys.
# Blank lines and `#` comments are ignored; a line without `=` is ignored.
#
# A VALUE IS LITERAL. Quotes are part of it, so PUBLISH_PATHS="skills" declares a
# path whose name includes the quote characters, and the run refuses naming it.
# Do not quote anything here.
#
# NOTHING IN THIS FILE CAN AUTHORIZE A PUSH. The confirmation is read from the
# process environment only; a CONFIRM_SHA or DRY_RUN key here is reported as
# ignored. That is by construction, not a check: the file is parsed, so an
# assignment in it is data and never an instruction.

# The public remote a promotion publishes to. SSH or HTTPS. No default is
# offered and nothing is inferred from an existing remote — a wrong value here is
# a push to somebody else's repository.
PUBLIC_TARGET=git@example.invalid:example-org/example-public.git

# The paths that ship. ONE LINE PER PATH — newline is the only separator a path
# cannot contain, so a name holding a space needs no quoting and gets none.
#
# This is an ALLOWLIST OF PATHS, and it binds at every depth. Anything not named is
# withheld with no action required to withhold it, and a path's NAME is never what
# withholds it: a file whose name reads as internal, sitting under a path named
# here, ships.
#
# AN ENTRY MAY BE NESTED, naming a directory or a single file, at any depth — there
# is no limit. `docs/guides` and `docs/index.md` are both valid entries. Where an
# entry names something inside a directory this list does not itself name, that
# directory is published PARTIALLY: the named descendants ship and every sibling
# not named, at that level or below it, is withheld by the same absence that
# withholds an unnamed top-level path.
#
# THE COST OF A PARTIALLY PUBLISHED DIRECTORY, stated plainly because it is the one
# thing nesting makes easier to get wrong: a new page added beside the published
# ones does not ship until a line here names it. That is the allowlist's failure
# mode — a missing page rather than a disclosure — and the dry run's `withheld
# under <dir>:` block is where you read it. Read that block.
#
# Do not name both a directory and something inside it. The run refuses: the
# narrowing the inner line implies is not in effect while the outer line ships the
# whole directory, and deleting the outer line later would change what ships
# silently.
#
# Every entry must exist in the tree at the tag being promoted or the run
# refuses. Replace these placeholders — they are the form, not a suggestion. The
# skill deliberately proposes no allowlist: run its scripts/history_probe_cli.py over
# every path you are considering withholding and decide on that evidence. See
# references/mirror-scope.md.
PUBLISH_PATHS=<a-path-that-ships>
PUBLISH_PATHS=<another-path-that-ships-nested-is-fine>

# The email the public commit and tag object are attributed to. A neutral,
# non-internal address by default, because the tag object's tagger line is one of
# the artifacts a publish carries whatever the path filter does. Overriding it to
# a real address is allowed and nothing here asks for an acknowledgement of that
# choice; see references/content-patterns.md for the gate a repository writes if
# it wants one.
PUBLISHER_IDENTITY=release-bot@example.invalid
